WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.007
Tonight, the cook texts three shops to ask who has the cheapest tomatoes.

2
00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:14.404
The greengrocer takes 300 milliseconds to reply, the market 120, and the farm stand 200.

3
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:19.679
In code, each shop is a Grocer, and price suspends until the shop replies.

4
00:00:19.679 --> 00:00:25.407
async starts one question, and hands back a Deferred at once: a pot with the lid on.

5
00:00:25.407 --> 00:00:26.771
await lifts the lid.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:36.690
Text all three first, then read the replies, and you wait only for the slowest: 300 milliseconds, not 620.

7
00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.347
The lesson's demo took 321, against 666.

8
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.556
Now a different job: telling each shop, we'll order bread today.

9
00:00:45.556 --> 00:00:48.505
No replies needed, so the cook uses launch.

10
00:00:48.505 --> 00:00:55.501
It starts a coroutine and returns a Job at once: a handle to wait for or cancel, with no value inside.

11
00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:58.679
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

12
00:00:58.679 --> 00:01:03.225
launch returns at once, three times, and nothing waits for the replies.

13
00:01:03.225 --> 00:01:05.338
So when does the function return?

14
00:01:05.338 --> 00:01:06.875
And who's still texting?

15
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.366
Let's entertain the scary answer.

16
00:01:12.366 --> 00:01:13.759
Say it returns at once.

17
00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:18.969
The caller thinks the job is done, and serves dinner, while three texts are still out.

18
00:01:18.969 --> 00:01:21.999
If one of them fails, there's nobody left to tell.

19
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.963
The cook is clearing the table, and her phone, in a drawer somewhere, is still texting the shops.

20
00:01:27.963 --> 00:01:30.043
Someone has to wait for every text.

21
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:33.558
A kitchen already has that someone: the course.

22
00:01:33.558 --> 00:01:37.123
The starters don't go out until every dish in them is ready.

23
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:39.439
The cook can finish her own part early.

24
00:01:39.439 --> 00:01:41.993
The plate still waits for the slowest dish.

25
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.753
In code, the course is coroutineScope.

26
00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:47.373
It runs its block, the cook's own part.

27
00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:51.807
Every coroutine started inside becomes one of its dishes, a child.

28
00:01:51.807 --> 00:01:55.368
And the course going out is coroutineScope returning.

29
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.637
So here's callAhead.

30
00:01:57.637 --> 00:02:03.816
Inside coroutineScope, it launches one text per grocer, and its block ends right away.

31
00:02:03.816 --> 00:02:05.756
When does callAhead return?

32
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:12.564
At 300 milliseconds, when the slowest grocer has answered.

33
00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:18.773
The block was done at once, but the scope waits for every child, even the ones nobody awaited.

34
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.206
And if the block ends with a value, the value waits too.

35
00:02:23.206 --> 00:02:30.491
A scope that launches a 200 millisecond job, and then says 42, returns 42 after 200 milliseconds.

36
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:38.484
That's the promise coroutineScope makes to your callers: when a suspend function returns, the work it started is done.

37
00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:40.953
Nothing keeps running behind their back.

38
00:02:40.953 --> 00:02:42.249
No phone in a drawer.

39
00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:45.401
It's also why you can't call launch just anywhere.

40
00:02:45.401 --> 00:02:51.681
launch needs a scope: something that keeps track of the coroutines started in it, and becomes their parent.

41
00:02:51.681 --> 00:02:53.089
Here, that's the course.

42
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.440
Now one dish goes wrong.

43
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.516
The market's reply fails at 120 milliseconds.

44
00:02:58.516 --> 00:03:03.164
When does callAhead finish, and what happens to the other two texts?

45
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:08.558
It fails at about 120 milliseconds.

46
00:03:08.558 --> 00:03:16.523
The scope cancels the other two texts, waits for them to stop, and then hands the market's exception to its caller.

47
00:03:16.523 --> 00:03:18.185
Nothing is left texting.

48
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.255
Waiting for the children, and stopping the rest when one fails: the next lesson draws the family tree behind both.

49
00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:30.321
While it waits, coroutineScope suspends, so the thread is free.

50
00:03:30.321 --> 00:03:33.901
And because it's a suspend function itself, it nests.

51
00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:43.559
The lab's priceList asks about tomatoes and basil, each through allPrices with its own scope: six questions at once, ready in 300 milliseconds.

52
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:45.239
A few rules.

53
00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.347
Start everything, then await.

54
00:03:47.347 --> 00:03:57.379
An await straight after each async took 627 milliseconds in the demo, the full sum, and still passed a test that only checked the answers.

55
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.564
Use launch when you don't need the value: an async nobody awaits hides what you meant.

56
00:04:03.564 --> 00:04:06.269
And keep runBlocking in main and tests.

57
00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:15.839
It holds its thread while it waits, the cook back at the pot: eight lookups on two threads took 858 milliseconds that way, instead of 292.

58
00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:19.093
One limit: the overlap comes from waiting.

59
00:04:19.093 --> 00:04:26.009
Three dishes that each need 300 milliseconds of chopping, on one thread, take 900, async or not.

60
00:04:26.009 --> 00:04:30.764
async can't grow the cook more hands: it overlaps waits, not work.

61
00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:40.071
Here's callAhead in Kotlin, and its closest Java match: an executor in a try block, which waits for every task when it closes.

62
00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:43.446
But if one task fails, it doesn't stop the others.

63
00:04:43.446 --> 00:04:45.133
coroutineScope does both.

64
00:04:45.933 --> 00:04:48.914
So, what stops a function from returning early?

65
00:04:48.914 --> 00:04:49.866
coroutineScope.

66
00:04:49.866 --> 00:04:53.228
launch and async start the dishes and return at once.

67
00:04:53.228 --> 00:04:57.350
The course waits for every dish, and stops the rest if one fails.

68
00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.156
A suspend function should return only when its work is done.

69
00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.313
Quick check.

70
00:05:02.313 --> 00:05:11.695
Inside coroutineScope, the cook awaits a 300 millisecond price, and launches a 500 millisecond text that nobody awaits.

71
00:05:11.695 --> 00:05:13.824
When does the scope return?

72
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.318
At 500 milliseconds.

73
00:05:18.318 --> 00:05:22.872
The price is in at 300, but the course waits for every dish.
